{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. ___ from the bariatric surgery team. I understand you’ve had about a week of epigastric and left upper abdominal pain that feels similar to your prior marginal ulcer pain. Can you tell me what it feels like right now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:46:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It’s that sharp pain under my left ribs and it goes through to my mid back. It comes and goes, but last night it got bad again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:47:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Any vomiting, black stools, or blood in the stool?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:47:55",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No vomiting. No black stools or blood."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:49:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "How about reflux, pain with swallowing, or chest pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:49:25",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No. Just the stomach pain and my usual nausea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:50:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Are you taking any NSAIDs like ibuprofen or naproxen? Any smoking or alcohol?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:51:05",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No NSAIDs. I don’t smoke or drink."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:52:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "And your G-tube feeds—are you still running them at 60 mL an hour for about 12 hours a day?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:52:55",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, 60 an hour. They’ve been going okay. I’m also eating by mouth."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:54:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "On exam your belly is soft, and I’m seeing some granulation tissue around the tube site—kind of a small raised area—but no signs of an acute abdomen. Given your history, we’re worried about a recurrent marginal ulcer, even though you’ve been on high-dose Protonix and sucralfate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:55:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Does that mean I need another scope?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:56:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not necessarily during this short stay. First we’ll stabilize symptoms and make sure there’s nothing dangerous like a perforation or obstruction. We’re going to start IV Protonix twice a day and continue sucralfate, and we’ll keep you NPO at first to rest the stomach."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:57:35",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "NPO again… I’m worried the pain will get worse if I don’t eat."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:58:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I hear you. The goal is to calm the irritation down. We’ll give IV fluids and a daily vitamin infusion—what we call a banana bag—with thiamine and folate while you’re not eating."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2171-02-06 17:00:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about pain control? I’m scared it’ll spike overnight."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2171-02-06 17:01:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We can start a PCA so you can control small doses for pain. We’ll watch closely for nausea, because opioids can worsen it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2171-02-06 17:02:05",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I get nauseated easily. Can I still have Zofran?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2171-02-06 17:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes—ondansetron as needed. We’ll also check labs, including nutrition labs, since absorption can be tricky after bypass."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2171-02-06 18:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "They said I’m going for an X-ray. What are you looking for?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2171-02-06 18:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "A plain abdominal X-ray—often called a KUB—to look for free air that could suggest a perforation, and to see the bowel gas pattern for obstruction."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2171-02-06 19:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your KUB is reassuring: normal bowel gas pattern, no free air, no obstruction. It does show a moderate amount of stool in the colon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2171-02-06 19:21:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s constipation causing this?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2171-02-06 19:22:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It may contribute to discomfort, but given your history and the way the pain feels, we’re still treating this like possible recurrent ulcer pain. The X-ray mainly helps us rule out emergencies."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2171-02-06 20:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your bloodwork looks stable overall—white count is normal, kidney and liver tests look okay, and lipase is normal. Your hemoglobin is a bit low, and your iron and ferritin are low, which fits iron deficiency."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2171-02-06 20:11:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that from bleeding from an ulcer?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2171-02-06 20:12:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Right now we don’t see signs of active bleeding—no black stools, no drop in blood counts during this evaluation. After gastric bypass, iron deficiency can happen from absorption issues. We’re going to adjust your iron supplementation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2171-02-06 20:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Iron pills upset my stomach. Is there another way?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2171-02-06 20:15:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re switching you to a liquid iron formulation and dosing it twice a day. We’ll also keep you on your bariatric vitamins—chewable multivitamin, calcium with vitamin D, and weekly B12."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2171-02-06 21:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The PCA is helping the pain, but I feel queasy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2171-02-06 21:06:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a common side effect. If you’re getting nausea or dry heaving, we may need to back off narcotics and use other strategies. I’m going to ask the chronic pain service to see you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2171-02-07 07:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How was the night—pain and nausea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2171-02-07 07:41:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I had dry heaves. The pain is a little better, but the nausea was awful."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2171-02-07 07:42:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Given the dry heaving, we’re going to stop the narcotics and hold the PCA. We’ll keep the IV Protonix twice daily and sucralfate. The pain team will help adjust your non-opioid regimen."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2171-02-07 07:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "If you stop the pain meds, won’t the pain come back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2171-02-07 07:45:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll still treat pain—just in a way that doesn’t trigger the nausea cycle. One change we expect is increasing your nortriptyline at bedtime, which can help with chronic abdominal pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2171-02-07 09:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The pain team mentioned changing my antidepressants. What’s happening with that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2171-02-07 09:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They recommended increasing nortriptyline to 100 mg at bedtime from 50 mg. They also recommended stopping Effexor. We’ll communicate their recommendations to your psychiatrist, Dr. ___."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2171-02-07 09:33:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m nervous about stopping meds. Will I feel withdrawal?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2171-02-07 09:34:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a fair concern. We’re following the pain service plan and coordinating with your outpatient psychiatrist. If you notice mood changes, anxiety spikes, or other symptoms, tell us right away and we’ll reassess."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2171-02-07 11:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "One more result: we checked H. pylori antibody testing, and it’s negative. That makes an H. pylori-driven ulcer less likely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2171-02-07 11:11:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So why do I keep getting these ulcer symptoms if I’m doing everything right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2171-02-07 11:12:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "After Roux-en-Y, the connection between the stomach pouch and small bowel can be prone to irritation in some people even without NSAIDs or smoking. The best-proven steps are exactly what you’re doing: high-dose PPI, sucralfate, and avoiding NSAIDs and tobacco."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2171-02-07 13:00:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m actually hungry now. Can I try something?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2171-02-07 13:01:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a good sign. Let’s start with bariatric Stage 3 diet. If you tolerate it without worsening pain or nausea, we’ll keep advancing slowly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2171-02-07 15:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I kept the Stage 3 down. No vomiting. Mild nausea but way better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2171-02-07 15:31:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Excellent. We’ll continue Stage 3 for now and keep your tube feeds cycled as you’ve been doing—60 mL per hour for a total of about 760 mL over 12 hours daily—along with your medications."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2171-02-07 18:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The tube site has that little bump. Is it infected?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2171-02-07 18:21:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It looks like granulation tissue—overgrowth of healing tissue—rather than an infection. There’s no fever and your labs don’t suggest infection. If you notice spreading redness, foul drainage, or worsening pain at the site, let us know."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2171-02-08 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How are your symptoms today—pain, nausea, and how you’re doing with the diet?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2171-02-08 08:06:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Pain is much better. I’m not nauseated now. I’m walking around fine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2171-02-08 08:07:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. Since you’re stable and tolerating Stage 3, we can plan for discharge later today. You’ll continue pantoprazole 40 mg twice daily and sucralfate 1 gram four times daily."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2171-02-08 08:08:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Do I take the sucralfate by mouth or through the tube?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2171-02-08 08:10:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You can take it by mouth as prescribed. At home you’ve also used it via the G-tube—continue following the instructions you’ve been given for your regimen. The key is four times a day, spaced out from other meds when possible."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2171-02-08 08:12:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the nortriptyline—100 at night now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2171-02-08 08:13:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, 100 mg at bedtime. We’re also stopping Effexor per the pain service plan, and we’ll make sure your psychiatrist receives the note so your outpatient team can follow you closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2171-02-08 10:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What diet do I go home on? I don’t want to trigger the pain again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2171-02-08 10:21:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Go home on bariatric Stage 3 and advance to Stage 4 and then Stage 5 as tolerated. Continue your cycled tube feeds at 60 mL/hour for 12 hours daily as supplementation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2171-02-08 10:23:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Do I need to crush all my pills again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2171-02-08 10:24:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes—crush all pills unless you’ve been specifically told not to. It helps with absorption and reduces the risk of irritation after bypass."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2171-02-08 12:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What should I watch for that means I need to come back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2171-02-08 12:06:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Come back or call if you have fever over 101.5, chest pain, shortness of breath, severe abdominal pain, pain not relieved by your plan, severe nausea or vomiting, severe bloating, inability to eat or drink, or concerning drainage or redness around the tube site."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2171-02-08 13:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will the iron make me constipated? The X-ray said I had a lot of stool."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2171-02-08 13:41:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It can. If you get constipation, you can use a stool softener like Colace twice daily as needed until your bowel pattern is back to normal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2171-02-08 15:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m relieved it’s better, but I’m still worried it’ll come back as soon as I’m home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2171-02-08 15:11:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That worry makes sense given your history. The plan at home is the same ulcer-protection strategy that helped here: pantoprazole twice daily, sucralfate four times daily, strict avoidance of NSAIDs, and gradual diet advancement. We’ll have you follow up with Dr. ___ to monitor symptoms and nutrition."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2171-02-08 16:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Before you go, let’s review your discharge meds: pantoprazole 40 mg twice daily, sucralfate 1 gram four times daily, ondansetron as needed for nausea, nortriptyline 100 mg at bedtime, liquid iron 300 mg twice daily, calcium with vitamin D, weekly B12, and a chewable multivitamin with minerals."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 68,
      "time": "2171-02-08 16:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. No NSAIDs, crush pills, Stage 3 diet and advance slowly, and keep the tube feeds cycled. I can do that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 69,
      "time": "2171-02-08 16:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. You’re stable, walking, and tolerating intake, so we’ll discharge you home now. If anything worsens, don’t wait—call or come in."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2171-02-06 16:38:00",
    "admission_end": "2171-02-08 16:33:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with prior laparoscopic Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and gastrostomy tube feeds, with a history of marginal gastrojejunal ulcers, was admitted to the bariatric surgery service for one week of recurrent epigastric/left subcostal pain radiating to the back with baseline nausea. She was managed with bowel rest (initial NPO), IV pantoprazole twice daily and sucralfate, IV fluids and daily vitamin supplementation, and pain control initially with PCA; narcotics were discontinued due to nausea and dry heaving, and chronic pain service adjusted her regimen by increasing nortriptyline to 100 mg nightly and stopping Effexor with communication to her psychiatrist. Abdominal radiograph showed no free air or obstruction and moderate stool burden. Nutritional labs demonstrated iron deficiency (low iron and ferritin), and iron supplementation was adjusted to liquid iron. Symptoms improved and she tolerated a bariatric Stage 3 diet, with plans to advance as tolerated and continue cycled G-tube feeds at 60 mL/hr for 12 hours daily. She was discharged home ambulatory and stable on PPI, sucralfate, antiemetic as needed, updated pain regimen, and bariatric vitamin/iron supplementation with surgical follow-up.",
    "problems": [
      "Abdominal pain, epigastric",
      "Gastrojejunal ulcer, unspecified as acute or chronic, without mention of hemorrhage or perforation, without mention of obstruction",
      "Bariatric surgery status",
      "Gastrostomy status",
      "Polycystic ovaries",
      "Morbid obesity",
      "Nausea alone",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified",
      "Anxiety state, unspecified",
      "Long-term (current) use of other medications"
    ]
  }
}
